      "text": "The greatest common divisor (GCD) of $5!$ and $\\frac{8!}{3!}$ can be expressed as $\\frac{\\text{GCD}(8!, 3!)}{\\text{GCD}(5!, 3!)}$.\nUsing the fact that $\\text{GCD}(a, b) = \\text{GCD}(a, b - a \\cdot \\text{GCD}(a, b - \\frac{a}{b}$), we get:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{GCD}(8!, 3!) &= \\text{GCD}(8!, 8!) - 8!\\cdot \\text{GCD}(8!, 8!) \\\\\n&= 8! - 8! \\cdot (2^3 \\cdot \\text{GCD}(4!, 2^3 - 8 \\cdot \\text{GCD}(4!, 4!)) \\\\\n&= 8! - 8! \\cdot 2^3 \\\\\n&= 8! - 672 \\cdot 8! \\\\\n&= 8! (1 - 672) \\\\\n&= 8! \\cdot (-671) \\\\\n&= -51840.\n\\end{align*}\nSimilarly, we have $\\text{GCD}(5!, 3!) = \\text{GCD}(5!, \\frac{5!}{3!}) = \\text{GCD}(5!, 4! - 4!) = 4! = 24$.\nHence, the GCD of $5!$ and $\\frac{8!}{3!}$ is $\\frac{\\text{GCD}(8!,3!)}{\\text{GCD}(5!,3!)} = \\frac{-51840}{24} = \\boxed{-2160}$.\nThe answer is: -2160",
